avfilter: add bwdif_videotoolbox, bwdif as a Metal compute kernel

Upstream has bwdif for CUDA and Vulkan, not for Metal, so on Apple
platforms a GPU deinterlace runs yadif while a CPU one runs bwdif, and
the picture depends on which path a session gets.

The kernel does the integer arithmetic of bwdifdsp.c on the integer
samples, held in floats. A float holds every integer up to 2^24 exactly,
and for samples of up to 10 bits every intermediate stays below that, so
the output equals CPU bwdif sample for sample, edge rows and the
end-of-stream field included. Deeper formats are refused.

The wrapper is vf_yadif_videotoolbox.m with bwdif's options (mode
send_frame / send_field, default send_field; parity; deint) and the
frame handling of yadif_common.c. It submits each frame to the GPU once,
all planes in one command buffer, and handles its failure paths like the
yadif_videotoolbox patch. A NULL texture or a missing command buffer stops
the frame, and a command buffer that ends in error is logged. Formats the
kernel cannot run (packed, deeper than 10 bits, more than two channels per
texel, planes under 3x4) are refused when the output is configured, before
an output pool is allocated.

Checked with framemd5 against CPU bwdif on 1080i TFF and 480i BFF
MPEG-2, testsrc2 at 1080 and 38x10, NV12 and P010, send_field and
send_frame, parity auto and forced. Every frame is identical. On an M1 a
1080p59.94 field costs 2.19 ms against 1.90 ms for yadif_videotoolbox in
the same run.

Written by classicjazz for AetherEngine issue 1102.
